<div id="nozzlealign_tab">
  <div class="row-fluid">
    <div class="span7">
      <div class="nozzlealign-view">
        <img data-bind="attr: {src: overlayUrl}" class="nozzlealign-frame">
      </div>
      <div class="alert" data-bind="visible: !cameraPresent()">
        <strong>The nozzle camera is not ready.</strong>
        <span data-bind="text: cameraMessage"></span>. Plug it in and put it on the
        bed with the lens up. Nothing here works without it.
      </div>
      <p class="muted">
        The blue crosshair is the target. The red circle is the nozzle bore
        that the run will steer on. Yellow means the score is too low to use.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-group">
        <button class="btn" data-bind="click: refreshPreview">Refresh</button>
        <button class="btn" data-bind="click: toggleLive,
                css: {'btn-primary': live}">Live</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="span5">
      <h4>Calibrate</h4>
      <div class="alert alert-error">
        <strong>This drives the toolhead down towards the camera.</strong>
        Put the camera on the bed with its lens up, clear of clips and clamps, and
        make sure the search area in the settings fits your bed. Stop the run if
        anything looks wrong. The nozzle never goes below the floor set in the
        settings.
      </div>
      <p class="muted">
        Put the camera anywhere on the bed, lens up. The run homes, sweeps
        the bed at Z90 until the toolhead comes into the picture, closes in
        on it, descends and finds the nozzle bore. It then sweeps Z for the
        sharpest picture, steers each nozzle onto the same pixel and
        reports the offset that puts both nozzles on one spot. It checks
        that the nozzle it measures is the active one.
      </p>
      <p data-bind="text: cameraText, visible: cameraText" class="text-success"></p>
      <p class="text-error" data-bind="text: readyText, visible: readyText"></p>
      <div class="btn-group" data-bind="visible: canCalibrate">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" data-bind="click: run,
                enable: !running() &amp;&amp; isReady()">Calibrate</button>
        <button class="btn btn-danger" data-bind="click: abort,
                enable: running">Stop</button>
      </div>

      <h5>Progress</h5>
      <pre id="nozzlealign_log" class="nozzlealign-log" data-bind="text: log"></pre>

      <!-- ko if: result -->
      <h5>Result</h5>
      <table class="table table-condensed">
        <tr><th>Stored T1 offset</th>
            <td data-bind="text: storedOffsetText"></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Nozzle 1 landed</th>
            <td data-bind="text: correctionText"></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Offset that fixes it</th>
            <td data-bind="text: newOffsetText"></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Residual after centring</th>
            <td data-bind="text: residualText"></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Image scale</th>
            <td data-bind="text: scaleText"></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Camera</th>
            <td data-bind="text: cameraText"></td></tr>
      </table>

      <p>
        Write the offset, then calibrate again. The second run must report
        nozzle 1 landing within a few microns of nozzle 0.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-group" data-bind="visible: canCalibrate">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" data-bind="click: applyNew, text: applyLabel,
                enable: !writing() &amp;&amp; isReady()"></button>
      </div>
      <p data-bind="text: writeStatus, visible: writeStatus,
                    css: {'text-success': writeOk, 'text-error': !writeOk()}"></p>
      <p class="muted">
        Prefer the slicer instead? Put the numbers above into the tool offset
        there and write nothing to the firmware.
      </p>
      <!-- /ko -->
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
